# RLS-scope conversion manifest — consumed by scripts/check_rls_coverage.sh.
# Format: <path> <status>   status: pending | converted | exempt
#
# Every backend/src file (store/ excluded) that can reach an RLS-covered table
# (roster derived from backend/migrations_control/0083_consumer_rls.sql) — either via direct SQL
# or via a Store method with a _scoped sibling — must be listed. Flip an entry to
# 'converted' in the SAME PR that routes its queries through
# tenancy::scope::begin_account_scoped — the script verifies the call is actually
# present (one documented exception: Store::ocean_stats_scoped manages its own
# scoping internally — see the script header). A file whose RLS-table references
# are ALL deliberate non-scoped surfaces (org-tenant-pool-only queries, or the
# audited SECURITY DEFINER carve-outs) gets 'exempt' with a '# exempt: <why>'
# comment directly above it. Regenerate with: scripts/check_rls_coverage.sh --seed
#
# NOTHING TO SCOPE, reviewed and recorded (org-tenancy OPS-4 phase B.5a), now
# carried as 'exempt' with per-line rationales rather than as permanent
# 'pending' (MCP-surface PR). B.5a left these three 'pending' on the reading
# that 'exempt' was reserved for deliberate non-scoped SURFACES — which would
# have parked them there forever and held the gate's pending count off zero
# for files that need no work at all. 'exempt' is the status the script
# actually validates (a '# exempt: <why>' line directly above the entry) AND
# the only one `--seed` carries across a reseed, so the reasoning now lives
# where the tooling protects it. Each rationale is inline below; all three were
# re-verified against origin/main before the flip.
#
# Hand-added status note (org-tenancy OPS-4 phase B.5b): routes/
# {sessions,session_shares,teams}.rs reach coding_sessions /
# coding_session_shares / teams / team_members / team_keys /
# team_member_key_envelopes only through the Store trait, so they are listed
# here by the expanded (Store-aware) detection, not the direct-SQL grep.
#
# ── PERMANENT DOCUMENTED EXCEPTION (decided, not outstanding work) ───────────
# Exactly ONE consumer-plane query path will never be `converted`, by decision
# rather than by backlog: the reachable-graphs authorization backbone
# (`Store::list_reachable_graphs` / `Store::graph_reachable_by_user`, reached
# from backend/src/tenancy/reachability.rs and ~13 route files). Its predicate
# is deliberately MULTI-account — `graphs.account_id IN (SELECT account_id FROM
# account_members WHERE user_id = $1)`, ADR-0007 "Model B" as documented in
# backend/src/middleware/auth.rs: one user belongs to several accounts and sees
# across all of them in ONE query — so there is no single account_id to
# `SET LOCAL app.account_id` with. Scoping it is not "not done yet"; it is not
# expressible without changing product behavior. It therefore runs under the
# SECURITY DEFINER functions `resolve_reachable_graphs` /
# `resolve_graph_reachable_by_user` (migration 0084_bootstrap_scope_functions,
# founder-reviewed there by name), which re-implement the identical predicate.
#
# COMPENSATING CONTROL — because SECURITY DEFINER means RLS is NOT evaluated
# inside those function bodies, this one path has no database-level backstop:
# if the predicate ever drifted (a weakened/dropped account_members join), it
# would leak every graph to every caller silently. That risk is closed by
# backend/tests/reachable_graphs_parity_pg.rs, which asserts the functions
# return byte-identical results (same rows, columns and ORDER) to the ORIGINAL
# inline predicate — a verbatim pre-#284 copy — across a two-account/one-user,
# one-account/one-user, no-account/one-user and unreachable-third-account
# fixture, plus the team/departed-member paths, the LIMIT 500 boundary, and
# negative assertions that fail loudly if the user_id filter is dropped. It
# runs in CI in the `rls-probe-harness` job (.github/workflows/ci.yml), NOT
# only locally. Any future change to those functions must keep that suite
# green; a failure there is a live authorization defect, not a stale test.
backend/src/billing/enforcement.rs converted
# converted: the single plan-entitlement query (grant-seats PR). The only RLS-covered table it reaches is `grants`; the `plans` it JOINs is control-only (CONTROL_TABLES in check_schema_parity.sh) and carries no 0083 policy. This file deliberately contains NO `begin_account_scoped` call of its own — `current_plan` is generic over `sqlx::PgExecutor` precisely so the CALLER supplies the scope, and the one production caller, `routes::usage::get_usage_summary`, passes its already-open account-scoped transaction (`&mut *ctrl_tx`), which is where the `app.account_id` predicate is satisfied. That generic executor is load-bearing rather than incidental: extracting this query out of the handler is what stopped `tests/billing_grant_seats_pg.rs` from proving a re-typed COPY of it (review finding — the old duplicate meant a handler regression left the suite green), and the test reaches it by passing a plain pool, which is RLS-exempt only because CI connects as superuser. Residual risk stated rather than waved away: a FUTURE caller could pass an unscoped pool and lose RLS defence-in-depth — that caller would still be constrained by this query's own explicit `WHERE g.account_id = $1`, whose bound value comes from the authenticated `UserContext` and never from request input, so the failure mode is "lost a second layer", not "reads another account's grant". Any new caller must still pass a scoped transaction; this note is the reason, not a licence to skip it.
backend/src/billing/entitlement.rs converted
# converted: the Phase 4a period roll (Stripe invoice.paid renewal). RLS-covered tables reached: `grants` (read, to resolve the per-member allowance) and `member_allowance_periods` (the INSERT itself; 0161, so NOT on this gate's 0083-derived roster and therefore invisible to it — stated here rather than left to be inferred). `plans` is JOINed but is control-only reference data with no 0083 policy, and `account_members` carries no RLS at all. This file deliberately contains NO `begin_account_scoped` call: `roll_period` takes `&mut PgConnection` precisely so the CALLER supplies the scope, for a reason stronger than entitlement.rs's — the renewal MUST run in the same transaction as `routes::stripe_webhook`'s idempotency claim, and opening its own scope would put a commit boundary between the claim and the work and reintroduce the double-grant the claim exists to prevent. The one production caller, `routes::stripe_webhook::apply_invoice_paid`, passes its already-open account-scoped transaction (`&mut *tx`), which is where the `app.account_id` predicate is satisfied. Residual risk, stated rather than waved away: a future caller could pass an unscoped connection and lose the RLS layer — that caller would still be constrained by every query here carrying an explicit `account_id = $1`, whose value comes from the SECURITY DEFINER `resolve_stripe_customer_account_id` and never from request input, so the failure mode is "lost a second layer", not "wrote another account's allowances".
backend/src/billing/renewal.rs converted
# exempt: concierge, one-shot export tool (member-private-oceans PR 9). Every RLS-table reference (graphs, atoms, nodes, node_atoms, relationship_edge, node_promotions, meeting_transcripts, transcript_corrections, conversations, conversation_messages) is run against `tenant` — a connection this binary opens directly to ONE org's own tenant database via EnvSecretResolver (never the shared control/consumer pool `coralswarm_app` connects to). Tenant databases have whole-database isolation and no 0083 policies at all (migrations_tenant/0003_bootstrap_scope_functions.sql), so there is no app.account_id predicate to satisfy and nothing for begin_account_scoped to scope — same shape as tenancy/member_ocean.rs and workers/member_ocean_reconciler.rs above. This binary ALSO touches the CONTROL plane directly (`control`, a separate pool/connection): it reads `tenant_databases` (SELECT-only, unaffected by 0083's role split) and `org_revocations` (not on the 0083 RLS roster — a control-only table, see CONTROL_TABLES in check_schema_parity.sh) to gate the export, and inserts/updates `member_ocean_lifecycle_log` (also control-only, migration 0092, not RLS-covered) for the audit trail. None of those control-plane tables are in the RLS roster this script derives from 0083_consumer_rls.sql, so they do not themselves require `begin_account_scoped` scoping — but are named here so this rationale accounts for every database plane the binary touches, not only its RLS-table references.
backend/src/bin/export_member_ocean.rs exempt
