# exempt: the core of the one-shot `backfill_reef_judgement_from_history` (judgement-graph slice PR5) — an operator-run reconciliation binary, never mounted on any request path and never reached from a worker (grep `judgement_backfill::` — the only caller in the crate is backend/src/bin/backfill_reef_judgement_from_history.rs; backend/tests/judgement_backfill_pg.rs is the other). Its four direct-SQL tables (graphs, nodes, node_merges, reef_judgement) and three Store methods (list_merge_dismissals, get_active_judgement, write_judgement) all run PLAIN/unscoped on purpose, exactly as store/mod.rs's judgement-graph PLANE CONTRACT sanctions for a caller that connects as the ELEVATED role (MIGRATOR_DATABASE_URL on the control plane; the tenant owner credential, resolved via EnvSecretResolver, on a tenant DB — where 0083's policies do not exist at all). Unlike every other `exempt` entry here, this one is not left to review: `judgement_backfill::assert_policy_bypass` PROVES the connection bypasses RLS on `reef_judgement` against pg_class/pg_roles before any work begins and REFUSES otherwise, so a run under the least-privilege coralswarm_app role stops at the preflight with an actionable message instead of erroring one candidate at a time. That refusal has its own negative control (a throwaway NOBYPASSRLS role) in backend/tests/judgement_backfill_pg.rs, wired into CI. begin_account_scoped would be the WRONG conversion here regardless: this tool walks EVERY ocean in a database, across accounts, by design.
