# CORRECTED (RLS-pending-retraction-deletion follow-up). The previous
# rationale here said this gap was "harmless today (RLS is enable-only
# pre-OPS-4-role-switch; both planes still connect as the BYPASSRLS owner
# role)" — that was FALSE at the time it was checked: verified against
# production, `DATABASE_URL`'s user IS `coralswarm_app` and `rolbypassrls =
# f`, so the OPS-4 role switch had already happened (2026-08-04) and the
# unscoped form was a live, hard-failing bug on
# `DELETE /v1/graphs/{graph_id}/atoms/{atom_id}` (0083's strict
# `current_setting('app.account_id')` raises rather than returning zero rows
# — see that migration's header). `unpublish_atom` is FIXED: it resolves
# `account_id` via the `resolve_graph_account_id` SECURITY DEFINER bootstrap
# (migration 0084) and opens its owned transaction through
# `begin_account_scoped(pool, account_id)` instead of a bare `pool.begin()`
# — the R2-vs-Postgres commit ordering the module's own doc argues for is
# unaffected (`begin_account_scoped` is `pool.begin()` plus one extra
# `set_config`, nothing more; it does NOT require a caller-supplied
# transaction, only a resolved `account_id`).
#
# UPDATE (same follow-up, second pass): `revoke_source_publication` (the
# sibling verb, `POST /v1/graphs/{graph_id}/revoke-publication`) is NOW ALSO
# FIXED, identically — it resolves `account_id` from `source_graph_id` via
# the same `resolve_graph_account_id` bootstrap and opens its owned
# transaction through `begin_account_scoped` instead of a bare `pool.begin()`
# against `source_publications` (RLS-covered since migration `0111`, not
# `0083` itself — the table didn't exist yet when `0083` shipped). Confirmed
# empirically that this was live-broken even with `source_publications`
# completely EMPTY in production (nothing writes it yet, per
# `routes::nodes`'s own comment): Postgres evaluates the RLS qual while
# planning the statement, not lazily per matched row, so zero rows was never
# a mitigation. A file-wide sweep (`grep -n 'pool\.begin\|fetch_.*(pool)\|
# execute(pool)' backend/src/services/retraction.rs`) turned up no other bare
# `pool`-against-RLS-table access in this file — `unpublish_atom` and
# `revoke_source_publication` were the only two, and both are now fixed, so
# this file flips to `converted`. See `services::retraction`'s module doc,
# "RLS scoping" section, for the full account, including why `source_graph_id`
# (not `target_graph_id`) is the correct anchor to resolve from. Regression
# tests: backend/tests/retraction_deletion_rls_pg.rs
# (`unpublish_atom_succeeds_under_app_role_pool`,
# `revoke_source_publication_succeeds_under_app_role_pool`), wired into CI's
# `consumer-rls` job.
backend/src/services/retraction.rs converted
backend/src/services/corrections.rs converted
